{"id":"dc76ab5e-e070-4f68-9da4-5bce021c2722","object":"chat.completion","created":1785030546,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    # initial symbol counts\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    # Validate required symbols exist\\n    for req in required:\\n        if req not in symbol_counts:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{req}' not present in any node\\\")\\n    # initial retained set\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # Build children map\\n    children_map = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    # Determine processing order: depth descending, then node id ascending\\n    order = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in order:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove root\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        # Check if node has any retained children\\n        has_retained_children = False\\n        for cid in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                has_retained_children = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if after removal all required symbols still present\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        new_count = symbol_counts[sym] - 1\\n        if new_count == 0 and sym in required:\\n            continue\\n        # Remove node\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = new_count\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    # Collect retained ids sorted\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that processes a tree of nodes with given fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The task is to perform a grammar-guided node reduction, removing nodes that are removable while preserving required symbols and ensuring grammar validity (though grammar is not explicitly given, but we have symbols and hierarchy). The output must have `removed_node_ids` (list of strings in order of removal) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted list of remaining node ids).\n\nFrom the problem description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nWe need to simulate removal order: iterate nodes in order of decreasing depth, then by node ID as tie-breaker for equal depth. For each node, if it is removable, and removing it (i.e., its children would become orphaned? Actually parent-child relationship: if we remove a node, its children become children of its parent? In tree reduction, removing a node typically means its children are reattached to its parent. But the problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests that if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not being removed), then we cannot remove the node because it would break the tree structure (children would be lost). In typical tree pruning, you might remove an internal node and connect its children to its parent, but here the constraints say \"a node with retained children\" cannot be removed. So removal is only possible for leaf nodes (or nodes where all children are also removed? But then children would be removed before parent? Actually if we remove a node, its children would also be removed? The problem says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That implies that if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So removal only applies to nodes that have no retained children (i.e., either leaf or all children have been removed). Since we process deeper nodes first, children are considered before parents. So we can remove a node only if all its children have already been removed (or it has no children). That aligns with typical bottom-up removal.\n\nAlso, we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (somewhere). So we need to track which symbols are currently present. Initially, we have a set of symbols from all nodes. Removing a node removes its symbol. So we need to ensure that for every required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol that is retained (not removed). Since we are processing bottom-up, we can check before tentative removal: if after removing this node, each required symbol still exists in the remaining nodes (excluding this node). That is sufficient.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root\" - root id is \"root\"? In fixtures, root has id \"root\". Also required symbols include \"root\". So root is always required.\n\n\"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" When sorting candidates for removal order, we need to sort nodes with same depth by node ID? Actually the removal order is the order in which we decide to remove nodes. But the problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order is the sequence of node IDs as they are removed. For same depth, we need a deterministic order. The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That's the primary sort. Then \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for removal order, we should process nodes in order of decreasing depth, and for same depth, ascending node ID (or some deterministic order). But careful: \"stable tie break\" could mean when two nodes have equal depth, use node ID to break ties. That likely means sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we only actually remove a node if after removal the required symbols are still present.\n\nAfter processing all nodes (i.e., considering each node once in that order, and if removable and condition holds, we remove it), we get a set of retained nodes. Then we need to output `retained_node_ids` sorted (presumably ascending order as strings) and `removed_node_ids` in the order they were removed.\n\nNote: The case payload includes `nodes` list and `required_symbols` list. We must not use any other fields.\n\nWe need to raise ValueError for infeasible case? The problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" But what constitutes infeasible? Possibly if required symbols are not present initially? That would be infeasible. Also if the root is removable? But root is marked removable=false in fixtures, but if it were removable, we still cannot remove it as per rule \"Never remove the root\". So we should check that.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse the case payload: `nodes` list, `required_symbols` list.\n2. Build a map from node_id to node dict. Also build a set of all symbols present initially (for required symbols check).\n3. Validate: ensure required_symbols are subset of symbols from all nodes? If not, raise ValueError.\n4. Determine initial set of retained node ids: all nodes initially.\n5. Create a set of removed node ids (empty).\n6. Determine processing order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id ascending (string compare).\n7. For each node in that order:\n   - If node['removable'] is False, skip.\n   - If node['id'] == \"root\" (or is root?), skip (never remove root).\n   - Check if node has any children that are still retained (i.e., any node whose parent is this node's id and that node is not removed). If yes, skip (node with retained children).\n   - Compute the set of symbols that would remain if we remove this node: current symbols minus the symbol of this node (but if there are multiple nodes with same symbol, we need to ensure at least one remains; we can check by counting occurrences). So we need to maintain a multiset of symbols per retained nodes? Or just check if after removal, the symbol still appears among retained nodes (including duplicates). Since we only remove one node at a time, we can count occurrences. Initially compute a dictionary symbol->count of retained nodes. For each removal, subtract count for that symbol. If after subtraction, count for any required symbol becomes zero, then skip removal.\n   - If all conditions pass, remove the node: add to removed_node_ids list, update retained set and symbol counts.\n8. After loop, collect retained_node_ids sorted alphabetically (as strings).\n9. Return dict with `removed_node_ids` (list in order of removal) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted).\n\nEdge cases: Node IDs are strings; sort by string comparison for tie-breaking and final sort. Depth is integer.\n\nCheck fixtures: In first fixture, root depth 0, n1 depth 1 symbol s1 (non-removable), n2 depth 1 symbol s2 (removable), n3 depth 1 symbol s0 (non-removable), n4 depth 2 parent n2 (removable), n5 depth 2 parent n3 (non-removable), n6 depth 2 parent n1 (removable). required symbols: root, s1. Process deeper nodes first: depth 2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n5 not removable, skip. n4 removable: check children? n4 has no children (leaf). After removal, required symbols: root, s1 still present? root symbol present, s1 from n1 (non-removable) so yes. Remove n4. n6 removable: leaf, s0 removal? s0 is not required, so fine. Remove n6. Then depth 1 nodes: n1 not removable, n3 not removable, n2 removable: check children: n2 has child n4 which is already removed, so no retained children. After removal, required symbols: root and s1 (n1) still present. Remove n2. Then root depth0 not removable. So removed order: n4, n6, n2. retained: root, n1, n3, n5. sorted: n1, n3, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nCheck second fixture: similar structure but different utilities; but utility not used in decision? Actually \"utility\" field is present but we don't use it. The problem description mentions \"utility\" but only in the data; the decision is based on depth, removable, required_symbols, and parent-child constraints. So we ignore utility.\n\nThus algorithm is straightforward.\n\nNow produce the implementation as Python source in a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The whole response must be exactly that JSON object.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) function. Use standard library only. Input case is a dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Return a dict with keys \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nBe careful with string escaping. We'll generate the Python code and then JSON-encode it.\n\nLet's produce the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":2484,"total_tokens":4656,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1973},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}